Our numerical results show that the regulatory constraint can substantially change the shape of optimal terminal wealth while imposing moderate welfare costs in many parameter configurations. Large losses arise when the required compliance probability is high, even the smaller regulatory wealth threshold is high, or initial capital is limited. In these cases, providing protection absorbs a substantial part of the available budget and leaves fewer resources for utility smoothing and upside exposure. In heterogeneous investor pools, welfare losses are particularly pronounced when highly risk-averse investors dominate the pool. While risk aversion strongly influences the magnitude of individual welfare losses, unequal Pareto weights can lead to substantial disparities in their distribution. In particular, investors with smaller capital contributions can bear disproportionately large shares of the total welfare loss, whereas larger contributors may bear considerably less than their capital-proportional share. These disparities are especially pronounced when a large investor is less risk-averse than the remaining pool members. These findings show why aggregate welfare alone is insufficient to assess the consequences of regulation for a collective investment pool. We also compare collective investment with constrained stand-alone investment and find that the presence of less risk-averse investors can make participation attractive to more risk-averse investors.

