\paragraph{Pairs} We split each article published up to the end of 2019, the end of our training period, into passages, drop passages that occur verbatim in more than one article (mostly publisher boilerplate), and draw one pair of different passages per article, so that an article's partner passage never appears as a negative for another pair in the same batch. This gives 230,931 pairs, of which 2,048 are held out. Passages are cut at 128 tokens. No returns, prices or text from the validation and test periods enter at this stage.

\paragraph{Objective} A passage's vector $z$ is the mean of its last-layer token vectors, scaled to unit length. The first-position vector is not an option, since DeBERTa was never trained with a sentence-level task on that position. For a batch of $B=32$ pairs with vectors $a_i$ and $b_i$, the loss is the symmetric InfoNCE
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L} = -\frac{1}{2B}\sum_{i=1}^{B}\left[\log\frac{e^{a_i^{\top}b_i/\tau}}{\sum_{j}e^{a_i^{\top}b_j/\tau}} + \log\frac{e^{a_i^{\top}b_i/\tau}}{\sum_{j}e^{a_j^{\top}b_i/\tau}}\right], \qquad \tau = 0.05,
\label{eq:infonce}
\end{equation}
which pulls the two passages of an article together and pushes the other 31 articles of the batch away. The second force is what spreads the vectors apart, so we apply no whitening or other correction afterwards.

