The study of high-dimensional sample correlation matrix has attracted huge attention. As the entries of the sample correlation matrix are standardized, the statistics based on the sample correlation matrix share an advantage of scaling invariance. Plenty of literature have demonstrated the efficiency of sample-correlation based statistics in many statistical inference problems, such as testing the cross-sectional dependence of high-dimensional samples \cite{gao2017high,jiang2019determinant,zheng2019test,yin2021spectral,yin2023central,bao2024spectral} and estimating the number of factors in spiked matrix models \cite{fan2022estimating,yin2024inference}. Typically, all previous works leverage the spectral statistics of the sample correlation matrix, such as the linear spectral statistics (LSS) and the extreme eigenvalue. Hence, theoretical analysis of the asymptotic spectral properties of high-dimensional sample correlation matrices is important for statistical inferences.



From a practical perspective, the observed samples are often modeled by a VAR process in many empirical studies, while most of existing theoretical literature focus on the sample correlation matrix of independent samples. In this article, we analyze the asymptotic spectral properties of the sample correlation matrix generated by a high-dimensional VAR process. Given the observed data matrix $\bbX:=[X_1,\cdots,X_T]\in\mbR^{n\times T}$, we consider $X_t$ generated by an \(n\)-dimensional VAR($d$) ($d\in\mbN^+$ fixed) process as below:
\begin{align}
	X_t+\sum_{l=1}^da_lX_{t-l}=\bbGa\varepsilon_t,\label{Eq of AR process}
